    T98
        title       Raw-throw class: one general solution for construction trust and public refusals
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T131, @task.V3.T132]
        satisfies   [@plan.Diagnostics.O1, @plan.Runtime.O1]
        description
            One general solution for the raw-throw class: no public path of Flow, built or handed out, throws anything
            but a FlowError on any argument, and construction trusts only what it made.

            **Why now:** parked, not now (the user, 2026-11-19): the class reached its third instance (T81's failure
            limit, T94's sweep, T94's review) and was blocking core work; the user chose to leave the known instances in
            as inverted tests and solve the class once, later, with a general design instead of case by case. This Task
            holds everything the class owes so the core phases can proceed. Split in the breakdown review (the
            orchestrator, 2026-11-20): this Task is construction trust, and it starts P5 (every feature builds on
            constructed values); the error vocabulary (a closed `details.reason`, one name for a store refusal) went
            to @task.V3.T100.

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T81 (its full approach and requirements 1 to 19, in its comments), @task.V3.T94
            (its review of 2026-11-18 and the decisions in its comments), @convention.boundaries; @spec.Operations.R10,
            @spec.Operations.I19 (code:wiki/specs/v3/Operations.md); @spec.Types.R13,
            @spec.Types.I4 (code:wiki/specs/v3/Types.md).

            The linked Spec is pre-coding analysis: follow it as best-guess guidance for how to build, not a strict
            contract; it should help a lot. Where building shows it wrong, fix the Spec and log the change
            in `wiki/specs/specs.log`.

            **Supersedes:** see @task.V3.T131 (doors) and @task.V3.T132 (`intake`), which carry the code list; this Task
            closes them.

            **Risk:** the known instances stay red as `it.fails` tests titled by Diagnostics R1; any later Task that
            adds a public function inherits the gap until this lands. Run convergence here (threshold already passed):
            every known instance red first, then one redesign from scratch for the whole class, with T81's and T94's
            approaches as prior art only.

            Owes, from T81: definitions, machines and tokens recognised by the `Symbol.for("flow-state/Made")` mark on
            one shared frozen prototype plus the owner compare (@spec.Operations.R10), not by `~` marks; builders that
            capture their arguments unread; one complete Schema decode per entry through `walkerOf`; its planted faults
            `token-by-shape`, `builder-reads-early`, `decode-not-walker`. From T94's review: inert data that hands out
            callable functions (a bound contract's `textOfKey`, an operation's `make`) — taken off the public types or
            wrapped, with the surface walk entering inert data; the testing route walked too (`defineConfig(null)`);
            `run()`'s lost catch-all; the `no-mark-probe` lint missing bracket reads.

            May change: `src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md`, @spec.Types and @spec.Operations
            with `specs.log` notes. Evidence: this Task's comments.
        acceptance
            - Every inverted test of the class (titled by Diagnostics R1) is green and no longer inverted.
            - The surface test walks the root and testing routes and enters inert data: no reachable function is
              unwrapped or unregistered.
            - No `~owner`, `~flow` or `HandleId` remains in `src/`; a Flow-made value is recognised only by its
              `flow-state/Made` mark and the owner compare (@spec.Operations.R10).
            - No builder reads its arguments: each called with an all-traps-throw Proxy returns a value.
            - `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass; `nub run faults --only` catches this Task's planted faults.
            - Correctness: a corresponding fixture app exists that uses the feature (a whole app written the way a user
              would write it; see @convention.fixture-catalog for the apps and how to build them); a Story tests that
              fixture's feature, plus the Spec's Story of the wrong ending; a test runs the Story; type tests show the
              feature is typed fully and correctly, the way a user would expect (two-sided pins, no annotations needed
              in user code); each new rule has a planted fault that `nub run faults` catches. See the existing tests
              (`packages/flow-state-v3/tests/`, `fixtures/`) for how things are done now. Tests cover
              @spec.Operations.R10, @spec.Operations.I19, @spec.Types.R13, @spec.Types.I4; the Invariants its tests
              check after every step are @spec.Operations.I19 (tests T5, T6), @spec.Types.I4 (tests T7, T9).
        comment     2026-11-18T19:14:16Z
